"Find the five places near two orbiting bodies where a small object can keep pace with them, see why asteroids gather at two of them and why spacecraft can park, with a little help, at two others.",
"<strong>Keeping pace.</strong> A small object farther from the Sun than Earth normally takes longer than a year to go around, and one closer in takes less. But near Earth, Earth’s own pull changes that. At five special places, the <strong>Lagrange points</strong>, the pulls of the Sun and Earth together give an object exactly the pull it needs to go around in one year, keeping the same position compared with Sun and Earth. The same is true for any pair: the Sun and Jupiter, Earth and the Moon.",
"<strong>Where they are.</strong> L1 is between the two bodies, L2 just beyond the smaller one, L3 on the far side of the big one. L4 and L5 are {#}° ahead of and behind the smaller body along its orbit, each making an equal sided triangle with the two bodies.",
"<strong>Stay or drift.</strong> At L1, L2 and L3 a small nudge grows: an object slides away like a ball balanced on a mountain pass, which the demo calls a saddle. At L4 and L5, if the smaller body is light enough, a nudged object wobbles around the point and stays. On the demo’s height map L4 and L5 are hilltops, so this is a surprise: as an object starts to slide off, it speeds up, and the turning of the frame (the Coriolis effect) bends its path sideways, so it circles the hilltop instead of leaving it. Thousands of <strong>Trojan asteroids</strong> share Jupiter’s orbit this way, gathered around Jupiter’s L4 and L5.",
"<strong>Parking spacecraft.</strong> L1 and L2 of the Sun and Earth sit about {#} million km from Earth, always in the same place compared with Sun and Earth. The SOHO observatory watches the Sun from L1; the James Webb Space Telescope (JWST) sits near L2, with Earth and the Sun always on the same side, behind its sunshield. Because they drift, these spacecraft fire small thrusters every few weeks to stay.",
